[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Memory Leaks, LeakCanary, and Garbage Collection Profiling in Android"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Memory management is critical for building high-performance Android applications. A memory leak occurs when an object is no longer needed by the application, but the garbage collector cannot reclaim it because a reference to it still exists. Over time, these leaks accumulate, leading to increased memory pressure, frequent garbage collection pauses, and eventually, the dreaded OutOfMemoryError (OOM)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How Garbage Collection Works in Android"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Android relies on the ART (Android Runtime) garbage collector to automatically manage heap memory. The GC uses a 'reachability' concept based on Garbage Collection Roots (GC Roots). GC Roots include local variables on thread stacks, active threads, static variables, and native references. If an object can be traced back to a GC Root through a chain of references, it is considered reachable and will not be collected."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Causes of Memory Leaks in Kotlin"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static References: Holding an Activity or Context reference inside a static field or companion object."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Long-running Threads and Coroutines: Spawning background tasks that implicitly capture references to the enclosing Activity or Fragment."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inner Classes and Lambdas: Non-static inner classes (like traditional Handlers or Adapters) maintain an implicit reference to their outer class."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unregistered Listeners and Callbacks: Registering event buses, RxJava subscriptions, or location listeners without unregistering them in lifecycle destruction methods."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Integrating LeakCanary"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"LeakCanary is a powerful open-source library by Square designed to automatically detect memory leaks in Android debug builds. To add LeakCanary to your project, include the dependency in your module-level build.gradle.kts file."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"dependencies {
    debugImplementation 'com.squareup.leakcanary:leakcanary-android:2.12'
}"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once added, LeakCanary automatically hooks into your app's lifecycle. It detects when Activities and Fragments are destroyed and watches for them to be garbage collected. If an object is not collected within a specific timeout, LeakCanary dumps the heap into an hprof file and analyzes it to display a notification highlighting the reference chain causing the leak."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Fixing Common Kotlin Leaks"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a scenario where a Singleton manager holds a reference to an Activity context. This prevents the Activity from being garbage collected when rotated."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// Leaky implementation
object LeakyManager {
    var context: Context? = null
}

// Fixed implementation using Application Context
object FixedManager {
    private var appContext: Context? = null

    fun initialize(context: Context) {
        appContext = context.applicationContext
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Garbage Collection Profiling with Android Studio"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"While LeakCanary alerts you to leaks during development, Android Studio's Memory Profiler allows deep-dive analysis of memory allocation and heap structures. Open the Profiler tab, click on the Memory graph, and select 'Capture heap dump'."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Class View: Sorts objects by class name, allowing you to track suspicious object counts after navigation actions."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference Tree: Inspects the incoming and outgoing references of a selected object to identify why a specific instance remains alive."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Force Garbage Collection: Use the garbage collector button inside the memory profiler to trigger a manual GC cycle before capturing a heap dump, ensuring transient objects are cleared."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Leak-Free Code"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always prefer application-level Context over Activity-level Context for long-lived components."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Clear view bindings in Fragment onDestroyView by setting them to null."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scope coroutines properly using lifecycleScope or viewModelScope to ensure automatic cancellation when the UI layer dies."}]
